CHECK:BIT_MACRO: Prefer using the BIT macro
#85: FILE: include/linux/lockdep.h:158:
+#define LOCKDEP_FLAG_TERMINAL_NESTABLE	(1 << 1)

Defines like: 1 << <digit> could be BIT(digit).
The BIT() macro is defined via include/linux/bits.h::

  #define BIT(nr)         (1UL << (nr))

WARNING:SINGLE_STATEMENT_DO_WHILE_MACRO: Single statement macros should not use a do {} while (0) loop
#100: FILE: include/linux/lockdep.h:334:
+#define lockdep_set_terminal_nestable_class(lock) \
+	do { (lock)->dep_map.flags |= LOCKDEP_FLAG_TERMINAL_NESTABLE; } while (0)

For the multi-statement macros, it is necessary to use the do-while
loop to avoid unpredictable code paths. The do-while loop helps to
group the multiple statements into a single one so that a
function-like macro can be used as a function only.

But for the single statement macros, it is unnecessary to use the
do-while loop. Although the code is syntactically correct but using
the do-while loop is redundant. So remove the do-while loop for single
statement macros.

CHECK:UNNECESSARY_PARENTHESES: Unnecessary parentheses around 'prev_type == LOCKDEP_FLAG_TERMINAL'
#133: FILE: kernel/locking/lockdep.c:3276:
+		if (DEBUG_LOCKS_WARN_ON((prev_type == LOCKDEP_FLAG_TERMINAL) ||
+			((prev_type == LOCKDEP_FLAG_TERMINAL_NESTABLE) &&
+			 (flags_is_terminal(class->flags) !=
+				LOCKDEP_FLAG_TERMINAL)))) {

Parentheses are not required in the following cases:

  1. Function pointer uses::

      (foo->bar)();

    could be::

      foo->bar();

  2. Comparisons in if::

      if ((foo->bar) && (foo->baz))
      if ((foo == bar))

    could be::

      if (foo->bar && foo->baz)
      if (foo == bar)

  3. addressof/dereference single Lvalues::

      &(foo->bar)
      *(foo->bar)

    could be::

      &foo->bar
      *foo->bar

